📗
  🚉 The server: a listening socket and a fixed pool of worker threads.

  Each worker accepts a connection on the shared listener, serves it to the
  end with ☎️, and comes back for the next one. The pool size is therefore the
  number of connections served at once; clients beyond it wait in the
  kernel's backlog. The threads are created once and joined once, because a 🧵
  that is released before it is joined aborts the process.

  🚉 is also what its connections ask about the world around them (📟).
